I’ll inspect the pattern metadata, the available crystallography workflow, and any candidate structure library first. Then I’ll identify phases from peak families, build/refine the multiphase GSAS-II model, and verify that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and project all agree.

The chemistry is Mn–W–O from WO₃ + Mn₃O₄ at 800 °C, with an uncalibrated Cu Kα Bragg–Brentano scan. I’m treating product tungstates, residual oxides, and Mn–W–O intermediates as live candidates; the next pass is peak-family analysis against the frozen structure index, not assuming a single 1:1 product.

The dominant family is unequivocally wolframite MnWO₄ (P2/c): its low-angle sequence at 15.48, 18.44, 23.67, 24.19, 29.98/30.42, and the dense high-angle pattern all match. A second coherent family remains—especially near 19.86–20.39°, 26.56°, 33–35°, and 55–60°—so I’m moving to a one-phase GSAS-II refinement to separate true unindexed peaks from profile/intensity errors before testing additions.

Adding monoclinic WO₃ lowers Rwp from 28.94% to 21.69% and carries ~18 wt%, so that phase is real. Several of the strongest “leftover” peaks are actually allowed WO₃ reflections whose intensities are extremely sensitive to the coarse published W/O coordinates; I’m now comparing clean WO₃ coordinate starts before declaring a third phase, as the residual-family rule requires.

The remaining clean peaks line up especially well with the Fe₂WO₆/Pbcn framework after Fe→Mn transfer: it uniquely predicts the 20–21°, 25–27°, 31–33°, 46.5°, 54.5–55.1°, 59.2°, and 65–66° family. I’ve generated a reaction-balanced candidate set (Mn₂WO₆, Mn₃WO₆, and Mn₃W₂O₉ in distinct plausible symmetries) and am now testing each as a third phase with comparable scale and cell relaxation.

The first naïve scale fits collapsed several transferred phases before their cells could move, so those are not rejections. I’m coarse-adapting each prototype lattice against the residual first, then only allowing scale refinement after its reflections are aligned; this directly tests whether Pbcn or a competing symmetry explains the whole family rather than a few overlaps.

The dominant MnWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃ models are secure. I’m now testing the coherent leftover reflection family against the chemically economical residual Mn₃O₄ precursor and the strongest transferred tungstate prototypes after comparable lattice adaptation; this is the decisive third-phase check.

The precursor hypothesis does explain much of the residual family, but only after 7–10% anisotropic cell distortion and very broad lines; Mn₂O₃ can imitate it similarly. That is too large to accept as a physical refinement, so I’m treating these as topology screens rather than identifications and checking the generator’s allowed perturbations plus direct indexing of the unassigned peaks.

Within the generator’s physical ±0.5% lattice-mismatch range, none of the Mn oxides accounts for the residual family; their apparent matches disappear unless the cell is distorted far beyond what this dataset permits. The family therefore needs a distinct Mn-rich tungstate framework, and I’m indexing only its clean, non-overlapped lines before returning to prototype transfer.

Direct indexing admits several low-symmetry cells because many lines overlap the two accepted phases, so I’m not selecting a cell by figure of merit alone. I expanded the balanced prototype set to Mn₂WO₅ and chemically closer Mn₃W₂O₈/Mn₃W₂O₉ frameworks; each is being screened with the same cell/profile freedom so a flexible decoy does not win by unequal treatment.

The equal-footing screen is still evaluating the low-symmetry candidates; those have more cell degrees of freedom and are the slowest. I’m retaining candidates only if the fitted metric stays near the source topology and the same model improves a full GSAS-II refinement, not merely this coarse residual correlation.

A decisive candidate emerged: an Mn₂WO₅ model transferred from orthorhombic Mn₂AsO₅ (Pnnm) fits the entire residual family with only ~0.4–1.6% axis changes, whereas alternatives need much larger metric distortions and fit worse. I’m now testing that adapted structure inside the full three-phase GSAS-II project; acceptance still requires stable nonzero scale and clean indexing of all residual peaks.

The Mn₂WO₅ scale remains stable at about 6–7 wt% in full refinement and its Pnnm cell converges near 8.685 × 8.922 × 6.312 Å. A simultaneous common-profile fit goes unphysical, confirming that the broad residual family is phase-specific; I’m separating Mn₂WO₅ size/strain from the common instrument before refining coordinates.

The transferred phase passes the scale/cell test. Refining its W position gives a small reproducible gain; Mn coordinates are not determined (large shifts with essentially no fit improvement), so I rolled that trial back. I’m now testing the oxygen substructure while holding the physically bounded broadening and will retain it only if bond geometry and replayed fit both improve.

The accepted-phase coordinate refinements are behaving physically and reproducibly: dominant MnWO₄ W/O shifts lower Rwp, and monoclinic WO₃ gives a smaller additional gain. The Pnnm phase remains stable through these changes. I’m moving to phase-by-phase width/texture tests now; calibration will be frozen until those are settled to avoid the zero/height correlation.

Phase-separated broadening lowers Rwp substantially while staying within the generator’s physical ranges: WO₃ carries high microstrain, MnWO₄ is moderately sized/weakly strained, and Mn₂WO₅ is very broad. Uniaxial trials only give small gains except one clearly unphysical runaway, which I rejected. I’m now checking the finite set of allowed preferred-orientation axes.

Preferred orientation is absent for MnWO₄ and WO₃ (ratios refine essentially to 1), but the broad Pnnm component has a clear [001] March–Dollase response. Its unconstrained ratio lands just beyond the allowed range, so I’m capping it at the physical 1.40 limit and replaying the full grid before accepting it.

The three-phase model is now well established: MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and an orthorhombic Mn₂WO₅-type phase. I’m doing the final residual-family and physical-structure checks, then I’ll replay the saved GSAS-II project and generate the submission files directly from that replay.

The residual check exposed a useful distinction: most mismatches sit under accepted phase families, but a weak isolated line near 25.42° and several poorly placed/intense lines warrant one more structural audit. I’m tracing those against the generator’s reference library before freezing the model; this is exactly where a plausible transferred framework can otherwise masquerade as a satisfactory minor phase.

The fitted orthorhombic cell is internally plausible but not close enough to the first Mn₂AsO₅ parent to treat that parent as uniquely established. I’m therefore widening the comparison to every accessible A₂BO₅ framework, substituting Mn/W consistently and testing distinct symmetries on equal, volume-normalized footing. This is a bounded framework test, not an open-ended phase hunt.

The equal-footing screen is running across 136 ordered A₂BO₅-derived models (33 symmetry settings represented). I’m ranking them on the two-phase residual first; only the few genuinely competitive topologies will be taken into full GSAS-II refinement.

The broad screen is still computing; it is buffered, so no partial rankings are visible yet. The search remains confined to the reaction-balanced Mn₂WO₅ hypothesis and will stop at the shortlist rather than proliferating phases.

This exhaustive pass is slower than the earlier hand-picked comparison because several low-symmetry cells have five nonlinear lattice variables. I’m allowing it to finish once; the payoff is a defensible symmetry shortlist and a clear answer on whether the 25.42° line belongs to the Mn₂WO₅ framework.

The exhaustive framework pass remains active and is the only long computation in the workflow. Once it returns, the downstream work is short: full-project trials for the leaders, physical-distance checks, then the immutable replay/export.

I stopped that pass because its brute-force lattice optimizer was spending most of its time on clearly poor low-symmetry cells. I’m replacing it with a two-stage screen: a fast uniform-volume/profile ranking over all 136 models, followed by full anisotropic adaptation only for the leaders. The scientific comparison is unchanged, but this avoids hours of uninformative optimization.

The fast screen is decisive: all six leading frameworks are Pnnm arsenate/phosphate analogues, with a single lower-symmetry P2₁/c relative in seventh place; the next unrelated topology is far worse. I’m now giving that P2₁/c alternative and the leading Pnnm coordinate starts the same anisotropic lattice treatment, specifically to test whether the 25.42° line is a symmetry clue.

The lower-symmetry test is materially better: after anisotropic adaptation, P2₁/c drops the residual-screen Rwp from about 14.20% (best Pnnm) to 11.87%, and its full three-phase GSAS-II trial already reaches 12.57% before phase-specific broadening or texture. I’m promoting P2₁/c as the leading Mn₂WO₅ model and will now refine it under the same physical bounds used for the other phases.

The first profile refinements improved Rwp to 7.84% with physical values (Mn₂WO₅ size ≈0.097 μm, strain ≈3929 ppm). A simultaneous isotropic size/strain fit tried to trade those into negative strain, so I rejected it. I’m now testing the generator’s allowed uniaxial strain directions; that should resolve the broadening without accepting an unphysical covariance shortcut.

The heavy-site test improved the fit modestly while preserving sensible W–O distances. Free Mn positions gave larger numerical gains but moved by roughly 0.5–1 Å and created 1.81–1.82 Å Mn–O contacts, inconsistent with the expected Mn(II)-oxide framework and the generator’s perturbation scale, so those trials are being rolled back. I’m checking oxygen sites one at a time from the physical W-refined model.

With the correct third-phase symmetry in place, the dominant MnWO₄ coordinates now refine by only 0.02–0.04 Å and lower Rwp to 6.96%. The two WO₃ W sites also give reproducible gains with 0.055–0.072 Å shifts, consistent with a two-site RMS perturbation near the stated range. I’m finishing the weaker WO₃ oxygen-site tests individually and will retain only physically small, reproducible movements.

A physical audit caught an important rollback: the inherited WO₃ CIF from the earlier two-phase fit had 1.29 Å O–O and 1.43 Å W–O contacts. Those arose from unconstrained oxygen refinement before the hidden phase was identified. I’m rebuilding the three-phase project with the unmodified, physically clean WO₃ reference and carrying over only the validated cells/profile estimates; the current WO₃ oxygen trials are rejected.

The clean 1528915 WO₃ parent still carries a crystallographic 1.58 Å O–O contact, so it is only “clean” relative to the earlier over-refinement. A same-formula monoclinic reference, COD 2311041, has the equivalent ~423 Å³ cell and normal ≥1.72 Å nearest W–O geometry. I’m running that physically cleaner setting through the same full three-phase refinement; it was already slightly better in the earlier two-phase comparison.

The 2311041 WO₃ setting is both physically cleaner and slightly better fitting (Rwp 6.71% after heavy-site refinement). Its oxygen refinements either moved 0.19–0.29 Å for negligible gain or produced 0.9–1.7 Å shifts and short bonds, so all are rejected. The retained model now has normal interatomic distances; I’m making a final pass over phase sizes/strain and instrument terms from this physical structure.

The final residual-family audit now closes cleanly: every major residual maximum lies within 0.024° of an accepted phase reflection, including the formerly problematic 35.12° and 46.49° lines. Only the modest 25.42° line remains isolated (nearest modeled reflection 0.52° away), so it does not justify a fourth phase. I’m testing whether the remaining WO₃ intensity error is allowed texture in the physically clean setting, then I’ll freeze and replay.

One final physically motivated correction remains: the selected WO₃ record imports zero displacement parameters, while the synthetic measurement always uses a phase-wide Uiso between 0.003 and 0.020 Å². I’m selecting that scalar on a small fixed grid (and checking the other phases afterward); this changes only high-angle attenuation and avoids unconstrained atom-by-atom ADPs.

The phase-wide Uiso check converged to 0.006 Å² (MnWO₄), 0.010 Å² (WO₃), and 0.005 Å² (Mn₂WO₅), all inside the measurement model’s physical range, lowering Rwp to 6.26%. The final profile and texture parameters are stable under a last instrument replay. I’m now freezing `final.gpx`, auditing distances/widths, and regenerating the profile independently from saved atomic parameters.

The first verifier pass caught a serialization-only issue: I had re-saved the project with the general scientific Python, which pickled masked arrays under a NumPy alias the restricted replay loader intentionally rejects. I’m re-saving the identical project with GSAS-II’s own Python environment, then rerunning the verifier; no model parameter is changing.

The final project now passes independent GSAS-II replay on all 8,301 points with no review flags. The replay-derived fractions are 62.20 wt% MnWO₄, 21.36 wt% WO₃, and 16.44 wt% analogue-derived Mn₂WO₅. I’ve generated the CIFs, profile table, JSON, and report; I’m doing one last cross-file consistency check before handoff.

Analysis complete. The crystalline phase model is:

- MnWO₄ (wolframite, P2/c): 62.20 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c): 21.36 wt%
- Analogue-derived Mn₂WO₅ (P2₁/c): 16.44 wt%

